  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Sun May 10 2026\n\nCurrently, a broad trough is situated across the northeastern \nquadrant of the country with a shortwave across the Northern High \nPlains. At the surface, a frontal boundary is draped from the Great \nLakes into southern Kansas. Winds have turned northerly to \nnortheasterly for all locations across the forecast area. A few \nshowers now dot northern Kansas, just moving into our central Kansas \ncounties. This activity, which developed as a product of increasing \nWAA and moisture ahead of the shortwave, will continue to shift \nsouthward during the early morning hours. Limited instability aloft \nwill support showers with a few rumbles of thunder primarily \nacross south-central Kansas, though the inverted V sounding \nwould indicate the isolated chance for gusty winds under the \nstrongest convection. \n\nRain will exit the forecast area later this morning with clearing \nskies through the afternoon. Highs this afternoon will be just below \nnormal for this time of year with temperatures topping out in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s. Ridging will begin to build in behind the \nfront by Monday with temperatures rebounding into the upper 70s to \nlower 80s across the forecast area. By Tuesday, the ridge axis will \nextend across the Rockies placing us solidly under northwest flow, \ncontinuing our warming trend. Highs on Tuesday will reach well above \nnormal with temperatures in the 80s to lower 90s areawide. \n\nModel guidance shows a shortwave digging into the Great Lakes region \non Tuesday, dragging a weak cold front across the forecast area by \nTuesday night. Moisture will increase ahead of the boundary (PWATs \nbetween 1-1.5\") leading to isolated shower and thunderstorm chances \non Tuesday night in eastern Kansas. Otherwise, temperatures on \nWednesday will be slightly cooler behind the \"cold\" front, though \nstill 5-10 degrees above normal, with highs reaching into the lower \nto middle 80s.\n\nRidging will primarily dominate the pattern through the end of the \nweek with the ridge axis overhead by Thursday leading to well above \nnormal temperatures in the upper 80s and 90s. A minor perturbation \nin the pattern could lead to some isolated shower chances on \nThursday night, otherwise we're not looking at any kind of \nsignificant pattern change till the end of the forecast period when \na trough approaches the Northern Plains leading to increasing rain \nand storm chances for next weekend.",
